  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1155 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe weather system will continue its slow passage from the desert\nsouthwest and across the southern plains Wednesday and Thursday.\nFor Wednesday the cold sector of the system will still be passing\nacross the eastern panhandles during the morning hours. This will\ncontinue the high chance for rain and thunderstorms in the \neastern panhandles. There will remain the threat for low top\ntropical supercells within the cold sector as it moves across the\neastern panhandles. This means that there is the risk for\nnocturnal to morning tornados even if the chances are very low. As\nfor the western panhandles there is a high chance for dry air to \nmove in behind the cold sector. This would serve to clear out the\nmoisture leading to calmer weather within the dry sector with a\nlow chance for rain shower and thunderstorms. This dry sector\nwould move eastward to encompass most of the panhandles by the\nafternoon to evening. Still, moisture will be wrapping around \nthis system so the northwestern panhandles will see an increasing \nchance for rain showers and thunderstorms Wednesday evening. This \nwrap around will then most likely move to the south and east \nthrough the overnight and all of Thursday. This will reintroduce a\nmedium chance for more rain showers and thunderstorms across the \npanhandles for Thursday. As this will be secondary moisture \nrainfall accumulations for Thursday will most likely be lower, \nespecially when compared to Tuesday through Wednesday morning.\n\nCome Friday the weather system will most likely be ingested within\nthe broader flow aloft and quickly shunted off to the northeast.\nThis will likely wrap up the rain during Friday morning as the\nmoisture finally departs. That will leave the rest of Friday as a\ncalmer and fairer weather day. \n\nAs for the weekend the pattern becomes less certain but overall it\ndoes favor higher pressure returning to the southern plains. This\nmakes it more likely than not for the upcoming weekend to have\nfair and calmer weather.",
